    caseText:
      "<p>Volt Hydropower Development Corporation Limited (VHDCL) developed the Volt Dam Project in Yazali, Arunachal Pradesh. The project began in the year 2003. The project was originally scheduled to be commissioned in 2007, but due to delays, on account of disputes in land acquisition, protests by environmentalists and political uncertainty, the project could be completed only in 2012, five years late from the originally planned commencement date. It has built a 1,900 MW Volt Hydro Power Complex, that comprises of Volt Dam &amp; 1,000 MW Volt Hydro Power Plant (250MW&times;4), Ujwal HEP (400 MW), and Volt PSP (500 MW).</p> <p>The Volt Dam is built across the Brahmaputra River. At a height of 1,000 ft, it is a large sized multi-purpose high rock and earth-fill embankment dam, with a reservoir capacity of 5 cubic kilometers. The 1,000 MW Volt Hydro Power Plant was commissioned in 2012, with variable speed features which can optimize the round-trip efficiency under varying water levels in its reservoirs. Power is distributed to 7 northern eastern states, including home state Arunachal Pradesh. This will also afford irrigation to the northeastern states and also supply millions of gallons of drinking water per day for around 2 million people living in this region. The company earns revenue by providing power to the north eastern states. As per agreement, home state Arunachal Pradesh gets 20% of the generated power free. The total expenditure for this project was USD 4.5 billion; it has substantially increased by 120% due to project delays. Since 2012-13, which was the first year of operation, VHDCL has been a profit-making company. The union government owns 75% stake and the state government owns balance 25% stake of VHDCL.</p> <p>The Volt Dam has been the object of protests by environmental organizations and local people of the region. The protest was against the displacement of town inhabitants and environmental consequences of the weak ecosystem. An estimated 4,000 families have been displaced on account of this project. It led to protracted legal battles over resettlement rights and, ultimately, resulted in the project's delayed completion despite the fact that land acquisition was started in 2003. However, as per the latest reports all the displaced families have</p> <p>been resettled in the New Volt Township area that has been developed nearby. The resettlement efforts started as early as 2004 at the commencement of the project. The company could acquire alternate land at a much cheaper rate as compared to current market prices. This township has now become a semi ultra-modern hill station with good road connectivity, healthcare and education facilities. All this was done as part of the project cost. The increased economic activity around this township created alternate employment for the local population. Despite all these measures few human rights activists claim that around 100 families who also lost their land to the dam project have not been officially recognized to be part of the group entitled to resettlement benefits.</p> <p>Environment activists have sounded alarm over the scale of the project as it is an earthquake prone area. They have also raised concerns about the riverine ecosystem that lives within the river. Following this the project came under intense public and governmental scrutiny; this forced the management to include adequate safeguards. The company&rsquo;s engineering team modified the design and construction of the dam keeping in mind the fragile ecosystem and risk of earthquakes in that region. This improved the dam&rsquo;s sustainability to withstand these risks and improved its longevity.</p> <p>The project has received the requisite regulatory certifications and has won many international awards. The company has also undertaken many CSR initiatives to conserve the surrounding fragile forests and natural ecosystem, soil and water conservation through rain water harvesting, building rural infrastructure and making health and education affordable to locals.</p>",
